    {
      "power": "SNEAKY",
      "cs_class": "SneakyPower",
      "cs_line": 24,
      "ported": false,
      "rust": null,
      "reason": "Multiplayer-only by construction, so the guard is false for every reachable single-player state — there is nothing to flash. C# `SneakyPower.AfterCardPlayed` fires only when `cardPlay.Card.Owner.Creature != base.Owner`, and `CardModel.Owner` is a `Player` (`CardModel.cs:334`), not a `Creature`: monsters own no cards, so the `!=` means \"a DIFFERENT PLAYER played an Attack\", never \"a monster attacked\". `Sneaky.cs:19` sets `MultiplayerConstraint => MultiplayerOnly` (C# `CardFactory.FilterForPlayerCount` drops it from single-player pools; Rust mirrors that in `spec_index::is_multiplayer_only`), `Sneaky.cs:30` is the only site that applies the power, and Rust has no allies in production — every `state.allies.push` sits inside a `#[cfg(test)]` block, so there is no second card owner to compare against. Porting it would add an unreachable branch and a `Flash()` that could never fire. The C# oracle agrees the Rust no-op is faithful: the `card:SNEAKY_CARD` coverage trial is clean. Pinned by `sts2-content/tests/sneaky_is_multiplayer_only.rs`. GH #638."
    },
